    "confidence": 0.9,
    "explanation": "Both parties: this is TRP-05019 of 12 September 2026, from Anchorpoint to Junction 8, Bishan, and the claim is a route deviation.\n\nWhat the record shows. The platform's recommended route was 11.8 km and the route driven was 13.1 km, so the deviation is 1.30 km (13.1 minus 11.8, POL-RD-22). That is over the 1.0 km tolerance, so the 'within tolerance' no-refund outcomes in POL-RD-24(c) and (e) are unavailable. The GPS trace (53 fixes, 100% coverage, 30-second interval) confirms the vehicle left the recommended PIE/CTE line at the Adam Road exit and rejoined at Bishan Road; under POL-RD-22 the trace shows which route was taken and when rather than re-measuring the distance.\n\nTraffic justification. The LTA advisory for the recommended PIE segment at the trip time reports type NONE: 'No incident, roadwork or closure recorded for the queried segment and window. Speed bands 4-5 throughout.' POL-RD-23 justifies a reroute only on congestion, an incident or a closure, and NONE is none of those. The reroute is therefore not traffic-justified, and the driver's account of a slow PIE does not stand in for the traffic data that clause requires.\n\nThe applicable row and the notification question. With the reroute not traffic-justified and the deviation over 1 km, POL-RD-24 row (d) applies and pays the excess distance fare in full. The driver's advocate argues the case must go to a human reviewer because the chat log is impaired and whether the driver notified the rider (POL-RD-21) cannot be established. The chat export is indeed IMPAIRED - TRUNCATED_LOG, weight 0.6, only 2 of the 4 recorded entries and nothing after the pickup - and I accept that notification before the 17:04:15 deviation is not shown on it. But which row applies does not turn on notification: row (d) refunds the excess distance fare whether or not the rider was informed, and it is rows (a) to (c), all of which require a traffic-justified reroute, that turn on it. The notification point is therefore not a fact this ruling needs, so it creates no open question for POL-GEN-26 and does not force escalation. The driver's statement that he 'messaged the passenger and she said ok', and its timing, are likewise not needed to decide the row; the rider's point that the only exported messages predate the trip and concern the pickup is noted and does not change the outcome.\n\nThe amount. The excess distance fare is the deviation distance times the per-km rate, without surge: 1.30 km x 0.72 SGD = 0.936 SGD, rounded to 0.94 SGD (POL-RD-24). That is what the rider is refunded. The rider asked for 3.00 SGD; the policy's own measure of the overcharge is 0.94 SGD, and the driver's answer that the 3.00 SGD figure is untethered from the meter is accepted - the driver is not charged the difference.\n\nWho decides. The disputed value is 3.00 SGD, below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD line, so the AI ruling is final, and neither profile carries a fraud flag. POL-RD-25 requires confidence of 0.80 for a route deviation; the facts this ruling rests on are carried by the platform trip record and the LTA advisory, both at weight_multiplier 1.0, so the confidence cap is 1.0 and the ruling issues at 0.90. The chat log at 0.6 does not lower the cap because it carries no fact the ruling rests on (POL-GEN-02(2)). POL-RD-25's 2 km limb is not applied, route_deviation.thresholds.ai_ruling_max_deviation_applied being false.\n\nOutcome. PARTIAL_REFUND of 0.94 SGD to the rider; no driver compensation and no referrals."
  },
